alternatives), so no conditional branch epics; the gate is recorded via `gated_by` with `rewrite` impact on the productization epic. |\n| E002 → E004 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring blocked by all Implementations) | Documentation must state the validated model list and the runtime's support scope, which only exist once productization lands. |\n| E003 → E004 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring blocked by all Implementations) | Documentation must state the training quota setup and driver prerequisites validated by the training work. |\n| E001 ∥ E003 | Rule 14 (parallel) | Different components, different teams, no shared artifacts. Training support does not build on the serving campaign's outcome; both run concurrently. |\n\n## HLR Traceability Matrix\n\n| HLR | Priority | Requirement | Epic |\n|-----|----------|-------------|------|\n| HLR-1 | P0 | Validation campaign on physical AMD Instinct hardware establishes functional and performance parity data for vLLM serving of the supported model set, producing the go/no-go scope for productization. | RHAISTRAT-9020-E001 |\n| HLR-2 | P0 | A supported vLLM ROCm serving runtime is productized for the models the validation campaign clears. | RHAISTRAT-9020-E002 |\n| HLR-3 | P1 | PyTorch training on ROCm is supported for single- and multi-GPU jobs admitted through Kueue, with amd.com/gpu quota handling. | RHAISTRAT-9020-E003 |\n| HLR-4 | P2 | Documentation states supported AMD hardware, driver prerequisites, and the validated model list. | RHAISTRAT-9020-E004 |\n\nEvery HLR maps to exactly one epic; no orphaned requirements and no\npriority-split candidates — each HLR is a distinct capability on a distinct\nsurface, and none is a restatement of another.\n\n## Health Warnings\n\n- **Missing strategy sections**: the strategy has no Impacted Teams table,\n  Dependencies table, Given/When/Then acceptance criteria, or NFRs. Teams were\n  taken from the architecture-context owning-team fields and acceptance\n  criteria were derived from the HLR text; a reviewer may want the strategy\n  backfilled.\n- **Hardware access (open question 1)**: procurement or partner-lab access to\n  dedicated AMD Instinct nodes is unresolved. This is an externally-controlled\n  availability precondition, not an acceptance criterion and not an\n  Investigation — its resolution does not change which epics exist or what\n  they do, only when the campaign can execute. Recorded as a risk on the\n  campaign epic.\n- **Upstream ROCm build cadence (open question 2)**: AMD's runtime image\n  release cadence is outside our control and may not align with release\n  trains. Handled via tiered delivery (below) and recorded as a risk on the\n  productization epic.\n- **Passive components**: KServe needs no code change for ROCm (it is\n  accelerator-agnostic per architecture context); its keep-working validation\n  under AMD nodes is an acceptance criterion on the validation campaign,\n  owned by the same team. Training Operator likewise needs no code change;\n  its validation is an acceptance criterion on the training epic, owned by\n  the same team.\n- **Component mapping note**: the training epic spans two strategy components\n  — Distributed Workloads (active: Kueue quota handling for amd.com/gpu) and\n  Training Operator (passive: validation only). It is assigned the single\n  canonical component \"Distributed Workloads\" as the most-relevant name; the\n  Training Operator validation is carried as an acceptance criterion rather\n  than a separate epic because the same team owns both.\n- **Planning note (epic size)**: the validation campaign epic will likely run\n  above the ~2-week guideline in calendar time — it covers the full supported\n  model set plus hardware access lead time. Kept as one logical change: a\n  single campaign against one hardware allocation; a split would duplicate\n  the rig setup, environment bring-up, and hardware booking.\n  **Re-split trigger**: re-split the campaign into per-model-family batches\n  if the model set grows beyond what one lab engagement can cover or the\n  execution effort exceeds a sprint of measured runtime.\n\n## Tiered Delivery\n\nThe external dependency (AMD / upstream ROCm image release cadence, timing\nuncertain) permits partial value before productization:\n\n- **Tier 1 (independent value)**: the validation campaign (risk-reduction —\n  consumes upstream ROCm images as-is and produces the parity data set) and\n  the Kueue amd.com/gpu training support (independent user-facing value on\n  the training side).\n- **Tier 2 (gated)**: the vLLM ROCm serving runtime productization, gated by\n  the campaign's go/no-go scope; its timing additionally tracks upstream ROCm\n  image availability for the chosen base.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":31227,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8a6a655ed610a647","agent_id":"a87fa06e82bd33954","session_id":"9503e158-5f16-4bd9-b154-1147d771dd9b","uuid":"98d0b8f5-f076-4746-81c6-4caede9b49b6","timestamp":"2026-10-25T20:33:52.841Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-9020"}
