{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-9ff9db85-829c-40aa-ab78-dec4fb774520","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-be43509e191c3f64","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-9020-d0zyu10e/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-9020-E001.md","content":"# AMD Instinct ROCm vLLM serving validation campaign\n\n## Description\n\nThis investigation resolves the strategy's core unknown: whether vLLM serving\non AMD Instinct accelerators via ROCm achieves functional and performance\nparity with the productized CUDA builds for the platform's supported model\nset. It runs a validation campaign on physical AMD Instinct hardware and\nproduces the per-model go/no-go scope that determines what gets productized.\nUntil this question is answered, the productization work cannot be scoped.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Stand up a validation environment on dedicated AMD Instinct nodes with the\n  upstream ROCm runtime images and vLLM ROCm builds as published upstream.\n- Execute functional validation of vLLM serving for every model in the\n  supported model set, recording pass/fail per model.\n- Execute performance validation (serving throughput and latency) per model\n  against the CUDA baseline, using the platform's standard benchmark\n  methodology.\n- Validate that vLLM ROCm runtimes deploy and serve through the standard\n  KServe InferenceService path on AMD Instinct nodes (KServe requires no code\n  change; this confirms it keeps working).\n- Produce the campaign report: per-model functional results, performance\n  parity data, and a per-model go/no-go recommendation constituting the\n  productization scope.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- Given the supported model set, when each model is served with vLLM on ROCm\n  on AMD Instinct hardware, then a functional pass/fail result is recorded\n  for every model in the set.\n- Given the functional results, when serving throughput and latency are\n  measured, then performance parity data relative to the CUDA baseline is\n  recorded per model.\n- Given vLLM ROCm runtimes, when they are deployed as KServe\n  InferenceServices on AMD Instinct nodes, then deployment and inference\n  succeed through the standard serving path (KServe keep-working validation).\n- Given the collected data, when the campaign concludes, then a published\n  report states a go/no-go recommendation per model, defining the\n  productization scope for the serving runtime.\n\n## Risks\n\n- Hardware access: dedicated AMD Instinct nodes must be secured via\n  procurement or a partner lab; this availability precondition is\n  externally controlled and gates campaign execution, not campaign design.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- **HLR-1 (P0)** — full coverage: the campaign on physical AMD Instinct\n  hardware establishes the functional and performance parity data for vLLM\n  serving of the supported model set and produces the go/no-go scope for\n  productization.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":39071,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8a6a655ed610a647","agent_id":"a87fa06e82bd33954","session_id":"9503e158-5f16-4bd9-b154-1147d771dd9b","uuid":"163d977d-6870-497c-950d-c0be6adeae17","timestamp":"2026-10-25T20:34:07.866Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-9020"}
