{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9848bebdc78a2cc5","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9020\n3\tepic_count: 4\n4\tcritical_path_length: 3\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\trevised: false\n7\ttriage: null\n8\ttriage_rationale: null\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-9020 (AMD GPU / ROCm enablement for serving and training)\n11\t\n12\tStrategy spans four components, two owning teams (RHOAI Serving, Distributed\n13\tWorkloads), and HLRs from P0 to P2, anchored by a hardware validation campaign\n14\twhose outcome gates productization scope — not below-threshold and not\n15\tdocs-only, so the full decomposition path applies.\n16\t\n17\t## Epic List\n18\t\n19\t| Epic | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n20\t|------|-------|------|------|----------|\n21\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E001 | AMD Instinct ROCm vLLM serving validation campaign | Investigation | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n22\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E002 | Productized vLLM ROCm serving runtime for validated models | Implementation | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n23\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E003 | Kueue amd.com/gpu quota handling and PyTorch ROCm training support | Implementation | Distributed Workloads | P1 |\n24\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E004 | AMD ROCm enablement documentation | Implementation (docs-authoring) | RHOAI Documentation | P1 |\n25\t\n26\t## Dependency DAG\n27\t\n28\t```mermaid\n29\tgraph TD\n30\t    E001[\"E001 ROCm serving validation campaign (Investigation)\"] --> E002[\"E002 vLLM ROCm runtime productization\"]\n31\t    E002 --> E004[\"E004 AMD enablement documentation\"]\n32\t    E003[\"E003 Kueue amd.com/gpu training support\"] --> E004\n33\t```\n34\t\n35\t## DAG Justification\n36\t\n37\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n38\t|------|------|-----------|\n39\t| E001 → E002 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream Implementation) | The campaign's go/no-go output determines which models the productized ROCm runtime must support — the scope of the downstream implementation. The outcome is a per-model list (not ≤3 bounded alternatives), so no conditional branch epics; the gate is recorded via `gated_by` with `rewrite` impact on the productization epic. |\n40\t| E002 → E004 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring blocked by all Implementations) | Documentation must state the validated model list and the runtime's support scope, which only exist once productization lands. |\n41\t| E003 → E004 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring blocked by all Implementations) | Documentation must state the training quota setup and driver prerequisites validated by the training work. |\n42\t| E001 ∥ E003 | Rule 14 (parallel) | Different components, different teams, no shared artifacts. Training support does not build on the serving campaign's outcome; both run concurrently. |\n43\t\n44\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n45\t\n46\t| HLR | Priority | Requirement | Epic |\n47\t|-----|----------|-------------|------|\n48\t| HLR-1 | P0 | Validation campaign on physical AMD Instinct hardware establishes functional and performance parity data for vLLM serving of the supported model set, producing the go/no-go scope for productization. | RHAISTRAT-9020-E001 |\n49\t| HLR-2 | P0 | A supported vLLM ROCm serving runtime is productized for the models the validation campaign clears. | RHAISTRAT-9020-E002 |\n50\t| HLR-3 | P1 | PyTorch training on ROCm is supported for single- and multi-GPU jobs admitted through Kueue, with amd.com/gpu quota handling. | RHAISTRAT-9020-E003 |\n51\t| HLR-4 | P2 | Documentation states supported AMD hardware, driver prerequisites, and the validated model list. | RHAISTRAT-9020-E004 |\n52\t\n53\tEvery HLR maps to exactly one epic; no orphaned requirements and no\n54\tpriority-split candidates — each HLR is a distinct capability on a distinct\n55\tsurface, and none is a restatement of another.\n56\t\n57\t## Health Warnings\n58\t\n59\t- **Missing strategy sections**: the strategy has no Impacted Teams table,\n60\t  Dependencies table, Given/When/Then acceptance criteria, or NFRs. Teams were\n61\t  taken from the architecture-context owning-team fields and acceptance\n62\t  criteria were derived from the HLR text; a reviewer may want the strategy\n63\t  backfilled.\n64\t- **Hardware access (open question 1)**: procurement or partner-lab access to\n65\t  dedicated AMD Instinct nodes is unresolved. This is an externally-controlled\n66\t  availability precondition, not an acceptance criterion and not an\n67\t  Investigation — its resolution does not change which epics exist or what\n68\t  they do, only when the campaign can execute. Recorded as a risk on the\n69\t  campaign epic.\n70\t- **Upstream ROCm build cadence (open question 2)**: AMD's runtime image\n71\t  release cadence is outside our control and may not align with release\n72\t  trains. Handled via tiered delivery (below) and recorded as a risk on the\n73\t  productization epic.\n74\t- **Passive components**: KServe needs no code change for ROCm (it is\n75\t  accelerator-agnostic per architecture context); its keep-working validation\n76\t  under AMD nodes is an acceptance criterion on the validation campaign,\n77\t  owned by the same team. Training Operator likewise needs no code change;\n78\t  its validation is an acceptance criterion on the training epic, owned by\n79\t  the same team.\n80\t- **Component mapping note**: the training epic spans two strategy components\n81\t  — Distributed Workloads (active: Kueue quota handling for amd.com/gpu) and\n82\t  Training Operator (passive: validation only). It is assigned the single\n83\t  canonical component \"Distributed Workloads\" as the most-relevant name; the\n84\t  Training Operator validation is carried as an acceptance criterion rather\n85\t  than a separate epic because the same team owns both.\n86\t- **Planning note (epic size)**: the validation campaign epic will likely run\n87\t  above the ~2-week guideline in calendar time — it covers the full supported\n88\t  model set plus hardware access lead time. Kept as one logical change: a\n89\t  single campaign against one hardware allocation; a split would duplicate\n90\t  the rig setup, environment bring-up, and hardware booking.\n91\t  **Re-split trigger**: re-split the campaign into per-model-family batches\n92\t  if the model set grows beyond what one lab engagement can cover or the\n93\t  execution effort exceeds a sprint of measured runtime.\n94\t\n95\t## Tiered Delivery\n96\t\n97\tThe external dependency (AMD / upstream ROCm image release cadence, timing\n98\tuncertain) permits partial value before productization:\n99\t\n100\t- **Tier 1 (independent value)**: the validation campaign (risk-reduction —\n101\t  consumes upstream ROCm images as-is and produces the parity data set) and\n102\t  the Kueue amd.com/gpu training support (independent user-facing value on\n103\t  the training side).\n104\t- **Tier 2 (gated)**: the vLLM ROCm serving runtime productization, gated by\n105\t  the campaign's go/no-go scope; its timing additionally tracks upstream ROCm\n106\t  image availability for the chosen base.\n107\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-974e7d2825fb6355","agent_id":"a8a800091a5010188","session_id":"9503e158-5f16-4bd9-b154-1147d771dd9b","uuid":"9f467d06-f2d8-475e-8598-72af73aaff46","timestamp":"2026-10-25T20:36:34.045Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-9848bebdc78a2cc5","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-9020 decomposition"}
